---
import DocsPage from "../../components/DocsPage.astro";
import { freeRenderLimit } from "../../data/cloud-facts";
import { loadCloudDocs } from "../../lib/batch3";

const { snippets } = loadCloudDocs();
const perMinute = freeRenderLimit();
const description = "Render a ChartKit spec to SVG or PNG. Free, with a chartkit.dev mark. A key removes the mark.";
---

<DocsPage title="Cloud render" description={description} href="/docs/cloud">
  <p>
    ChartKit Cloud renders a spec for any agent or app. There is no signup. Free images include a small <code>chartkit.dev</code> mark, linked to <a href="/cloud">/cloud</a>. A valid API key removes the mark. This service draws the mark. <code>@chartkitdev/core</code> does not, and the core bundles do not include it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every render runs <code>validate()</code> and <code>repair()</code> first. Response headers carry the JSON Patch, the warning codes, and the error codes. <code>POST</code> also returns them as JSON. <code>render_chart</code> on <code>@chartkitdev/mcp</code> returns the image URL plus those repairs. The package is not published yet. Example URLs on this site use <code>https://cloud.chartkit.dev</code> unless the site build sets <code>PUBLIC_CHARTKIT_CLOUD_URL</code>.
  </p>
  <h2>GET /render</h2>
  <p>
    <code>spec</code> is URL-encoded JSON or base64url JSON. Omit <code>format</code>, or pass <code>svg</code>. SVG is the default: it skips rasterizing and stays sharp in chat. Pass <code>png</code> only when the host cannot display SVG, such as email, a GitHub README image, or some Slack clients. <code>w</code> and <code>h</code> set the pixel size. <code>scale</code> is the PNG zoom, from 1 to 4. Prefer 2 or higher when the format is PNG. <code>theme</code> is a preset name such as <code>soft</code> or <code>midnight</code>. <code>background</code> is a CSS color (<code>#ffffff</code>, <code>rgb()</code>, <code>hsl()</code>, <code>oklch()</code>, or <code>transparent</code>). This GET always includes the mark. A key on the query string is ignored, so an image URL stays free of secrets.
  </p>
  <figure class="code"><figcaption>html</figcaption><pre><code>{snippets.img}</code></pre></figure>
  <h2>POST /render</h2>
  <figure class="code"><figcaption>js</figcaption><pre><code>{snippets.fetch}</code></pre></figure>
  <p>
    The JSON body is <code>spec</code>, <code>format</code>, <code>w</code>, <code>h</code>, <code>scale</code>, <code>theme</code>, and <code>background</code>. Send a key only as <code>Authorization: Bearer</code>. The body includes <code>url</code>, <code>applied</code>, <code>warnings</code>, <code>errors</code>, and either <code>svg</code> or base64 <code>png</code>. A valid key stores the repaired spec and returns <code>/c/&lt;id&gt;.svg</code> (or <code>.png</code>) with no secret in the query. Without a key, a short spec keeps a <code>GET /render</code> URL and a spec longer than 1,800 characters is stored with the mark. Soft, Refined, and Editorial leave the plot transparent so a web page can show through. Cloud paints <code>#ffffff</code> instead, unless you pass <code>background</code> or the spec already sets <code>theme.tokens.background</code>. The query param wins over that token. Pass <code>background=transparent</code> to keep the plot clear. On a dark host, pass <code>theme=midnight</code> (light text on an opaque dark plate) or a dark <code>background</code> together with a dark theme. PNG uses the same plate.
  </p>
  <h2>POST /charts and POST /validate</h2>
  <p>
    <code>POST /charts</code> stores the repaired spec and returns <code>id</code>, <code>svgUrl</code>, and <code>pngUrl</code>. The same spec and the same mark state return the same id. A valid <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> stores it unmarked. <code>GET /c/&lt;id&gt;</code> serves that stored image and does not read a key. <code>POST /validate</code> does not draw. It returns the repaired spec, <code>applied</code>, and the remaining diagnostics.
  </p>
  <h2>Headers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>X-ChartKit-Repairs</code> and <code>X-ChartKit-Repair-Patch</code> (base64url JSON Patch)</li>
    <li><code>X-ChartKit-Warnings</code> and <code>X-ChartKit-Errors</code></li>
    <li><code>X-ChartKit-Watermark</code> is <code>1</code> or <code>0</code>. <code>X-ChartKit-Key</code> is <code>valid</code>, <code>invalid</code>, or <code>absent</code></li>
    <li><code>Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable</code> and an <code>ETag</code> on rendered images</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Errors and limits</h2>
  <p>
    A spec that repair cannot draw returns <code>422</code> and an image that lists the CK codes, for <code>GET</code>. <code>POST</code> returns the same codes as JSON. A missing or unreadable spec returns <code>400</code> and an error image. More than {perMinute} renders a minute from one IP returns <code>429</code>. The limit is the same with a key.
  </p>
  <h2>Keys</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/cloud">Remove the watermark</a> starts checkout. Pricing is coming soon. When checkout is available, <code>POST /stripe/webhook</code> issues one key per completed Checkout Session, and the success page shows that key. Send it as <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> on <code>POST /charts</code> or <code>POST /render</code>. Use the returned <code>/c/&lt;id&gt;</code> URL in an <code>img</code>. Do not put the key in that URL.
  </p>
  <figure class="code"><figcaption>js</figcaption><pre><code>{snippets.clean}</code></pre></figure>
  <p>
    Hosted keys and stored charts use Vercel KV or Upstash when <code>KV_REST_API_URL</code> and <code>KV_REST_API_TOKEN</code> are set. A Workers deploy reads those same variables from Worker secrets. Without them, the process keeps keys and charts in memory.
  </p>
  <p>PNG uses resvg wasm with Inter and Roboto bundled in the service. SVG text stays on the library font stack. Agents should omit <code>format</code> and attach the SVG. PNG is the fallback for a host that cannot show SVG.</p>
</DocsPage>
